    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=4f8993d7-7486-4f03-a125-59b813eb0331&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=472138810_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Associate Director of Development",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-08-05\n\nLocation: Crestview Hills\n\nBasic Purpose The Associate Director of Development manages and implements strategies to ensure proper identification, cultivation, solicitation and stewardship of major gift prospects. The Associate Director of Development manages all strategies related to securing philanthropic support from grants.  Core Values The successful candidate will be a visionary and collaborative leader who serves as a public ambassador for the advancement of the mission of the University and consistently demonstrates the Thomas More University core values of Hospitality, Humility, Service, and Stewardship. Core Competencies Exceptional verbal and written communication skills and the ability to prepare proposals, reports and correspondence. Strong organizational, supervisory, and interpersonal skills. Ability to manage multiple projects concurrently. Ability to utilize various software and databases connected to university fundraising operations. Understanding of NCAA compliance as it relates to donor/boosters engagement and responsibilities. Responsibilities: Develop and implement strategies to ensure proper identification, cultivation, solicitation and stewardship of major gift prospects capable of making gifts greater than $25,000. Coordinate with faculty, staff, and administrators to organize applications for grants from foundations, federal agencies, and other entities in collaboration with external consulting partners.  Assist in the leadership of Thomas More University’s planned giving program, developing and implementing systems and processes for increasing participation in planned giving, securing estate gifts, and enrolling donors in the Monsignor Murphy Legacy Society.  Advise the AVP for Institutional Advancement on a regular basis regarding Major Gift program activities, effectiveness and results. Manage the involvement of the President, senior leadership, trustees, and committee members in donor cultivation. Oversee the acknowledgment and recognition for gifts at various levels and implement programs to ensure fundraising goals are achieved at each giving level.  Engage regularly with faculty and Deans to ensure a working knowledge of content expertise, areas of research, and academic fundraising priorities/goals.  Manage a portfolio of approximately 30-50 major gift prospects and document contacts and solicitation plans. Average 15 face to face meetings per month with current donors, potential new donors and community leaders. Work with the Annual Giving and Advancement Services team to integrate and improve strategic prospect management systems.  Prepare written and oral presentations and proposals as needed. Travel as necessary to cultivate and solicit principle and major gifts. Generally support the University’s development program, including, but not limited to attendance at and help with fundraising, recognition, and alumni events. Assist staff as needed with additional development initiatives and undertake special projects and assignments as directed. Perform other duties as assigned or requested. Education, Specialized and/or Technical Knowledge Requirements  A bachelor’s degree required Minimum of five years demonstrated successful fundraising experience including personal gift solicitation is required. Transferable experience may be considered.  Experience with committee and volunteer management as well as prospect research is required.  Physical Job Requirements Tools and Equipment Used Personal computer, copier, fax/scanner, phone, and other typical office equipment Travel Often – more than 30% of the time (primarily in the Greater-Cincinnati region)  Physical & Mental Demands Frequently required to sit at a desk/workstation for long period of time Ability to work at a computer terminal for extended periods of time Digital dexterity and hand/eye coordination in operation of office equipment Light to moderate lifting and carrying of supplies, setting up tables and chairs, etc. Ability to speak to and hear employees/clients via phone or in person Body motor skills sufficient to enable incumbent to move around the office environment Ability to analyze data and other reports and make recommendations Mental requirements include: compare, decide, direct, problem solve, analyze, instruct, interpret",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=4f8993d7-7486-4f03-a125-59b813eb0331&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=472143068_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Director, Zembrodt Center for Entrepreneurship & Innovation",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-07-27\n\nLocation: Crestview Hills\n\nDirector Zembrodt Center for Entrepreneurship & Innovation (ZCEI) Division of Strategic Growth  Thomas More University   Position Summary Thomas More University invites applications for the position of Director of the Zembrodt Center for Entrepreneurship & Innovation (ZCEI). This is a full-time faculty appointment within the Robert W. Plaster College of Business that combines excellence in teaching with leadership of the University's entrepreneurship and innovation initiatives. The ZCEI Director serves as the chief academic and strategic leader of the ZCEI, advancing the University's mission by fostering an entrepreneurial mindset among students, cultivating partnerships throughout the regional business community, and expanding the University's reputation as the innovation destination for the Greater Cincinnati and Northern Kentucky region. The successful candidate will teach a standard four-course teaching load each semester and receive a two-course release per semester in recognition of the administrative responsibilities associated with directing the Center. Primary Responsibilities Academic Leadership Teach undergraduate and graduate courses in entrepreneurship, innovation, and related business disciplines. Develop and enhance an innovative entrepreneurship curriculum. Mentor students pursuing entrepreneurial ventures. Collaborate with faculty across disciplines. Center Leadership and Strategic Growth Provide strategic leadership for the ZCEI. Serve as the University's primary spokesperson for entrepreneurship and innovation. Position the University as a regional leader in entrepreneurship and innovation. Advancement and Philanthropy Partner with the Thomas More University Foundation and Institutional Advancement to cultivate donors and secure financial support. Engage in the procurement of grants to support the ZCEI, the College of Business, or related University areas when appropriate. Recruit, support, and engage with an external advisory board of key stakeholders in the entrepreneurial ecosystem of the region and/or proven entrepreneurs who might serve as potential investors in start ups who are engaged in formal relationships with the ZCEI. Entrepreneurial Venture Development Recruit and support promising startup companies. Provide mentorship and coaching in collaboration with the Advisory Board. Develop partnership agreements through revenue sharing, royalties, equity participation, or other mission-aligned investment models, as appropriate.   Regional Partnership Development Build relationships with investors, venture capital firms, chambers of commerce, economic development organizations, and entrepreneurial partners. Design and facilitate workshops, executive education, speaker series, and professional development University Leadership Serve on the University's Strategic Growth Committee and contribute to strategic initiatives. Required Qualifications A master’s degree in Entrepreneurship, Business Administration, Management, Innovation, or related discipline. Demonstrated success teaching entrepreneurship or related business disciplines. Experience leading entrepreneurship, innovation, incubation, or ecosystem initiatives. Excellent communication and collaboration skills. Preferred Qualifications A doctorate degree in a related field. Experience directing a university entrepreneurship center. Experience mentoring startups. Experience with fundraising, grants, or corporate partnerships. Personal Characteristics The successful candidate will be a visionary and collaborative leader who serves as a public ambassador for the Center and consistently demonstrates the Thomas More University core values of Hospitality, Humility, Service, and Stewardship.   About the Center Mission The Dr. Anthony ’65 and Geraldine ’66 Zembrodt Center for Entrepreneurship and Innovation will equip students from diverse disciplines with essential, real-world skills that empower them to succeed in any field they pursue. We help students identify opportunities for innovation, starting a business, and learn how to approach the world with a creative, resourceful, and entrepreneurial mindset. Vision To be the leading destination for innovation in the region and a hub for our students and community to develop and foster entrepreneurial thinking to start and grow businesses and as a way of life",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=4f8993d7-7486-4f03-a125-59b813eb0331&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=523463631_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Intl Student Services Coordinator / Designated School Official",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-11\n\nLocation: Crestview Hills\n\nInternational Student Services Coordinator / Designated School Official (DSO) Join Thomas More University in Welcoming the World Thomas More University is seeking an  International Student Services Coordinator / Designated School Official (DSO) to serve as a trusted resource for our international students and a key partner in the University’s commitment to student success, global education, and hospitality. This position provides leadership for international student services and F-1 immigration compliance while supporting students from admission through graduation. The Coordinator serves as the University’s primary operational resource for  F-1 regulations, SEVIS, SEVP requirements, and international student advising , while also helping create a welcoming and engaging campus experience. What You’ll Do Serve as a Designated School Official (DSO) and institutional resource for F-1 immigration regulations and SEVP compliance. Maintain accurate SEVIS records, Forms I-20, Form I-17 requirements, and required federal reporting and deadlines. Advise international students on maintaining F-1 status, enrollment, employment authorization, travel, academic changes, CPT, OPT, transfers, and program extensions. Monitor international student status and provide proactive outreach when academic, compliance, or personal concerns arise. Coordinate international student orientation and programs supporting cultural adjustment, engagement, retention, and student success. Support Graduate & Global Education programs, including residency weekends and related student and faculty coordination. Collaborate with Enrollment, Academic Affairs, Student Life, Athletics, Financial Aid, the Registrar, faculty, advisors, and other campus partners. Maintain Terra Dotta and other systems supporting international student services. Monitor changes in federal regulations and communicate their impact to University leadership and campus partners. Contribute to departmental projects, strategic initiatives, international partnerships, and operational improvements. What We’re Looking For Required Bachelor’s degree. Ability to become and maintain eligibility as a Designated School Official. Excellent organizational, communication, and interpersonal skills. Strong attention to detail and ability to manage confidential information. Demonstrated ability to collaborate effectively across departments. Preferred Experience with SEVIS and F-1 immigration regulations. Experience in higher education, international education, student services, advising, enrollment, or a related field. Experience with Terra Dotta or comparable international student management systems. Experience advising international students or coordinating student programs and events. A Thomas More Saint At Thomas More University, we believe how we serve matters as much as what we accomplish. The successful candidate will demonstrate the Characteristics of a Saint through: Service — Respecting and advancing the Catholic mission and identity of Thomas More University. Stewardship — Responsibly managing student records, institutional resources, regulatory requirements, and the trust placed in this role. Humility — Approaching students and colleagues with openness, respect, a willingness to listen, and a commitment to serving others. Hospitality — Creating a welcoming environment where students from around the world feel known, supported, valued, and at home. Why This Role Matters International students bring unique perspectives, cultures, and experiences that enrich the entire Thomas More community. In this role, you will help ensure that international students have both the compliance guidance and personal support they need to thrive.. If you are organized, relationship-oriented, globally minded, and passionate about helping students succeed, we invite you to join the Thomas More University community. This position requires occasional evening and weekend work, including participation in Global Education residency weekends approximately three times per year.",
      "department": ""
    },
    "https://workforcenow.adp.com/mascsr/default/mdf/recruitment/recruitment.html?cid=4f8993d7-7486-4f03-a125-59b813eb0331&ccId=19000101_000001&jobId=550799610_1&lang=en_US": {
      "title": "Bluegrass Regional Dual Credit Coordinator",
      "description": "Posted: 2026-09-23\n\nLocation: Crestview Hills\n\nAbout the Role: The Bluegrass Regional Dual Credit Coordinator plays a pivotal role in facilitating and managing dual credit programs that enable high school students to earn college credits while completing their secondary education. This position requires close collaboration with high schools, colleges, and other educational stakeholders to ensure program alignment, compliance with academic standards, and smooth operational processes. The coordinator will oversee student enrollment, monitor academic progress, and provide guidance to both students and educators to maximize the benefits of dual credit opportunities. Additionally, the role involves data collection and reporting to support program evaluation and continuous improvement. Ultimately, the coordinator ensures that dual credit initiatives effectively expand access to higher education and promote student success within the Bluegrass region. Minimum Qualifications: Bachelor’s degree from an accredited institution. Experience working in educational program coordination, preferably within dual credit or college readiness programs. Strong organizational and communication skills with the ability to manage multiple stakeholders. Proficiency in data management and reporting tools. Knowledge of secondary and postsecondary education systems and policies. Preferred Qualifications: Master’s degree in Education, Educational Leadership, Counseling, or a related field. Experience working directly with high school and college administrators and faculty. Familiarity with state and federal regulations governing dual credit programs. Demonstrated success in student advising or academic counseling. Experience with grant writing or program funding management. Responsibilities: Develop and maintain partnerships with high schools, colleges, and community organizations to support dual credit program implementation. Coordinate student enrollment processes, including eligibility verification, registration, and communication with students and parents. Monitor academic progress and provide support or intervention strategies to ensure student success in dual credit courses. Organize and conduct informational sessions, workshops, and training for educators, students, and families about dual credit opportunities and requirements. Collect, analyze, and report data related to program participation, outcomes, and compliance with institutional and state policies. Serve as a liaison between educational institutions to resolve issues and facilitate effective communication. Stay informed about changes in educational policies, funding, and best practices related to dual credit programs. Skills: The coordinator will utilize strong communication skills daily to engage effectively with students, parents, educators, and institutional partners, ensuring clear understanding of program requirements and benefits. Organizational skills are essential for managing multiple program components, including enrollment, data tracking, and event coordination, to maintain smooth operations. Analytical skills are applied in reviewing student performance data and program metrics to identify trends and areas for improvement. Problem-solving abilities are critical when addressing challenges related to student eligibility, course alignment, or inter-institutional coordination. Additionally, proficiency with technology and data management systems supports accurate reporting and compliance with educational standards.",
      "department": ""
    }
  },
